# PySpark Write — Complete Practical Guide

---

## Core Write Syntax

```python
df.write \
    .format("parquet") \
    .mode("overwrite") \
    .option("key", "value") \
    .partitionBy("year", "month") \
    .save("s3://bucket/output/")
```

Every write operation has these 5 building blocks: **format, mode, options, partitioning, and destination.**

---

## 1. Write Modes

Controls what happens when **data already exists** at the destination.

```python
df.write.mode("overwrite").parquet("s3://bucket/data/")
```

| Mode | Behavior | Use When |
|---|---|---|
| `overwrite` | Replaces everything at path | Full refresh / reprocessing |
| `append` | Adds new files, keeps existing | Incremental loads, streaming |
| `ignore` | Does nothing if data exists | Idempotent one-time loads |
| `error` / `errorifexists` | Throws exception if path exists | Default — safety net |

### ⚠️ Gotchas

```python
# DANGER: overwrite deletes the ENTIRE directory, not just matching partitions
df.write.mode("overwrite").parquet("s3://bucket/orders/")  # wipes everything

# SAFE: overwrite only specific partition
spark.conf.set("spark.sql.sources.partitionOverwriteMode", "dynamic")
df.write.mode("overwrite").partitionBy("date").parquet("s3://bucket/orders/")
```

Always set `partitionOverwriteMode = dynamic` in production when writing partitioned data — otherwise you'll wipe partitions you didn't intend to touch.

---

## 2. Output Formats

### Parquet — Your default choice for analytics

```python
df.write \
    .mode("overwrite") \
    .option("compression", "snappy")  # snappy / gzip / zstd / none
    .parquet("s3://bucket/output/parquet/")
```

- Columnar, compressed, schema-preserving
- Best for: **Athena, Glue, Redshift Spectrum, Spark-to-Spark**

---

### JSON

```python
df.write \
    .mode("overwrite") \
    .option("compression", "gzip") \
    .json("s3://bucket/output/json/")
```

- Human-readable, schema flexible
- Best for: **APIs, dead-letter queues, debugging output**
- ⚠️ Writes one JSON object per line (JSONL), not a JSON array

---

### CSV

```python
df.write \
    .mode("overwrite") \
    .option("header", "true") \
    .option("delimiter", ",") \
    .option("quote", "\"") \
    .option("escape", "\\") \
    .csv("s3://bucket/output/csv/")
```

- Best for: **Reporting, Excel exports, downstream systems that need CSV**
- ⚠️ No schema preservation — all types become strings on read

---

### Delta Lake — Production standard

```python
df.write \
    .format("delta") \
    .mode("overwrite") \
    .partitionBy("year", "month") \
    .save("s3://bucket/delta/orders/")

# Or write to a Delta table
df.write \
    .format("delta") \
    .mode("append") \
    .saveAsTable("orders")
```

- ACID transactions, schema enforcement, time travel
- Best for: **Lakehouse architecture, upserts (MERGE), audit trails**

---

### JDBC — Writing to Databases

```python
df.write \
    .format("jdbc") \
    .option("url", "jdbc:mysql://host:3306/mydb") \
    .option("dbtable", "orders") \
    .option("user", "admin") \
    .option("password", "secret") \
    .option("driver", "com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver") \
    .option("batchsize", "10000")       # rows per batch insert
    .option("numPartitions", "8")       # parallel write connections
    .mode("append") \
    .save()
```

- ⚠️ **`numPartitions` controls DB connections** — don't set too high or you'll overwhelm the DB

