## 3. Partitioning — The Most Critical Write Concept

### `partitionBy` — Hive-style directory partitioning

```python
df.write \
    .mode("overwrite") \
    .partitionBy("year", "month", "region") \
    .parquet("s3://bucket/orders/")

# Output structure:
# s3://bucket/orders/year=2024/month=01/region=US/part-0000.parquet
# s3://bucket/orders/year=2024/month=01/region=EU/part-0001.parquet
```

**Partition column selection rules:**
- Use **low-cardinality** columns (year, month, region, status)
- Never partition by high-cardinality columns (user_id, order_id) — creates millions of tiny files
- Ideal partition size: **128MB–1GB per partition file**

---

### `bucketBy` — Spark-managed bucketing (for joins)

```python
df.write \
    .mode("overwrite") \
    .bucketBy(64, "customer_id") \
    .sortBy("order_date") \
    .saveAsTable("orders_bucketed")
```

- Pre-shuffles data so joins on `customer_id` skip the shuffle stage entirely
- Only works with **managed tables**, not raw file paths
- Best when you repeatedly join two large tables on the same key

---

### `repartition` vs `coalesce` Before Writing

```python
# repartition — full shuffle, use to INCREASE or balance partitions
df.repartition(100).write.parquet("s3://bucket/output/")

# coalesce — no shuffle, use to REDUCE partitions (e.g., combine small files)
df.coalesce(10).write.parquet("s3://bucket/output/")

# Repartition by column — ensures same-key data lands in same file
df.repartition("region").write.partitionBy("region").parquet("s3://bucket/output/")
```

| Scenario | Use |
|---|---|
| Too many small files | `coalesce(n)` before write |
| Uneven partition sizes | `repartition(n)` before write |
| Want one file per partition | `repartition(col).partitionBy(col)` |

